  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 222 AM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A broad 1028mb surface high will be positioned to our \nwest over the Great Lakes region today. This will keep northerly \nflow across the region. Fortunately, the cool air coming in will be \nmore of a glancing blow with zonal flow aloft. Still, it'll feel \nplenty cool with highs lingering mainly in the 50s, except for a few \n60s across south-central Vermont. Mountain summits will likely be in \nthe upper 30s to mid 40s this afternoon. Abundant dry air will \npromote clear skies. We should radiate quite efficiently tonight \nwith these conditions, and that will bring overnight lows in the 30-\n40 F range.\n\nHigh pressure will start moving east Sunday, and south to southwest \nwinds return. A more moderate day in the 60s is expected for Sunday, \nwith a warmer night around 40-50 F. These warmer conditions will \ncome with some wind, though as pressure gradients tighten against \ndeparting high pressure and the next approaching system. Anticipate \n10-15 mph winds, and up to 20 mph in the Champlain Valley. The \nattendant frontal boundary will arrive pre-dawn through the early \nafternoon. The moisture is not great, but the dynamics of a strong \nupper jet and modest frontal support could produce a broken narrow \nline of rain. Larger synoptic scale motion will yield stronger winds \nbehind this front. Combined with the strength of cold air behind it, \nthe 50s forecast on Monday and 25-35 mph gusts across the region \nwill feel nippy. Advective cold will result in mid 20s to upper 30s. \nThe winds may still preclude frost formation in the Champlain \nValley, but plenty cool. Moisture quickly departs behind the frontal \nboundary, but if there's anything lingering, a few flakes or some \nriming is feasible for mountain terrain.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Strong northwest flow will continue through Tuesday with \ntemperatures feeling solidly like fall, before our air mass \nmoderates towards the weekend. Highs Tuesday will struggle to reach \n50 in the higher terrain with low to mid 50s elsewhere with breezy \nnorthwest winds as our flow pattern remains fairly confluent. Skies \nwill remain clear into Tuesday night with one of the coldest nights \nof the season expected. With a stout dry 700-500mb layer, strong \nradiational cooling will take place with many places falling into \nthe low to mid 30s. Some patchy frost will be possible in the \nsheltered locations, but with temperatures falling under clear \nskies, almost the entire region could experience a freeze, with the \nChamplain valley remaining on alert at this time. This will likely \nbe a curtain call for gardeners sensitive plants in the broad \nvalleys. \n\nTemperatures will slowly moderate back up towards the weekend with \nthe approach of a broad long wave trough associated with a deepening \nupper low over the Hudson Bay. Highs will warm to the upper 50s to \nmid 60s, with overnight lows in the upper 30s to mid 40s. A large \nscale cyclonic gyre will shift south from the interior of Canada \nleading to several shortwave passages beginning late next week into \nthe weekend. Daily diurnally enhanced showers will be possible \nstarting Thursday with increasing cloud cover into the weekend. In \naddition to increasing shower chances, each shortwave passage will \nbring with it some breezy to gusty winds to northern New York, and \nlikely some channeled flow in the Champlain Valley each day.",
